<SubSection title="Detail Design" id="Detail Design">
</SubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Introduction" id="Introduction">
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="What is Detail Design?" id="What is Detail Design?">
<p>Detail Design is the phase where conceptual ideas are transformed into fully specified, manufacturable products. This phase translates design intent into precise technical documentation that manufacturing, quality control, procurement, and other stakeholders can use to produce the product exactly as intended. Detail Design encompasses four critical components:</p>
<Enumerate>

    <Item><strong>Computer-Aided Design (CAD)</strong> - 3D models that define geometry</Item>
    <Item><strong>Detailed Engineering Drawings</strong> - 2D documentation with dimensions and tolerances</Item>
    <Item><strong>Analysis</strong> - Analytical and computational validation</Item>
    <Item><strong>Bill of Materials (BOM)</strong> - Complete list of parts, assemblies, and materials</Item>

</Enumerate>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Why Detail Design Matters" id="Why Detail Design Matters">
<p>Detail Design serves as the bridge between concept and reality. Without thorough detail design:</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Manufacturing cannot produce parts to specification</Item>
    <Item>Quality control cannot verify conformance</Item>
    <Item>Procurement cannot order correct materials</Item>
    <Item>Assembly teams cannot understand how components fit together</Item>
    <Item>Cost estimation becomes guesswork</Item>

</Itemize>
<p><strong>The fundamental principle</strong>: Detail Design documents <em>what</em> must be achieved (specifications, dimensions, materials) to enable others to determine <em>how</em> to achieve it (manufacturing processes, assembly sequences).</p>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Design Intent vs. Manufacturing Method" id="Design Intent vs. Manufacturing Method">
<p>A well-executed detail design specifies requirements without over-constraining the solution. For example, specifying "corrosion resistant in saltwater for 5 years" as a requirement allows manufacturing to choose from stainless steel, protective coatings, or other solutions. Specifying "must use 316 stainless steel" removes flexibility and may increase cost unnecessarily.</p>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Component 1: Computer-Aided Design (CAD)" id="Component 1: Computer-Aided Design (CAD)">
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Purpose of CAD Models" id="Purpose of CAD Models">
<p>CAD enables engineers and designers to develop product designs with accuracy, efficiency, and innovation. 3D CAD models serve as the master definition of product geometry and are used throughout the product lifecycle for:</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Design visualization and iteration</Item>
    <Item>Manufacturing process planning</Item>
    <Item>Assembly simulation</Item>
    <Item>Interference checking</Item>
    <Item>Documentation generation</Item>
    <Item>Prototyping and tooling development</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="CAD Best Practices" id="CAD Best Practices">
<h4><strong>Master the Fundamentals</strong></h4>
<p>A strong foundation in basic CAD skills is paramount, with proficiency required in 3D modeling, drawing, and dimensions for detailed and precise CAD designs. Before implementing advanced techniques, ensure mastery of:</p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Sketch constraints and relationships</Item>
    <Item>Feature creation and editing</Item>
    <Item>Assembly mates and constraints</Item>
    <Item>Part configurations and design tables</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Use Parametric Design</strong></h4>
<p>Parametric design enables easy modifications by linking parameters, ensuring that changes automatically propagate throughout the entire model. This approach:</p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Links dimensions through equations and relationships</Item>
    <Item>Allows rapid design iterations</Item>
    <Item>Maintains design intent when dimensions change</Item>
    <Item>Reduces errors from manual updates</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Bike Lock Example:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Link shackle diameter to required strength calculations</Item>
    <Item>Tie mounting bracket dimensions to lock body size</Item>
    <Item>Connect key dimensions to cylinder requirements</Item>
    <Item>Any change to shackle diameter automatically updates dependent features</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Implement Naming Conventions</strong></h4>
<p><p>Implement a standardized naming convention for files, layers, and components, as it promotes organization and ensures that team members can quickly locate and understand the various elements within the CAD project.</p>
<strong>File Naming Standards:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Use consistent prefixes: \verb|BL-| (Bike Lock), \verb|SH-| (Shackle), \verb|CYL-| (Cylinder)</Item>
    <Item>Include version numbers: \verb|BL-LockBody-v03.sldprt|</Item>
    <Item>Add descriptive names: \verb|BL-ShackleCap-HardenedSteel-v02.sldprt|</Item>
    <Item>Avoid spaces; use hyphens or underscores</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Layer/Feature Naming:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Use descriptive names: "Keyway-Cut" not "Cut-Extrude3"</Item>
    <Item>Group related features: "Mounting-Holes" for all mounting features</Item>
    <Item>Identify critical features: "CRITICAL-SealingSurface"</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Optimize Model Performance</strong></h4>
<p><p>As CAD models become more complex, optimizing them for performance becomes essential through techniques such as simplifying geometry, utilizing lightweight components, and strategically using levels of detail.</p>
<strong>Performance Optimization:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Suppress unnecessary features when not needed</Item>
    <Item>Use simplified representations for large assemblies</Item>
    <Item>Avoid overly complex surfaces where simple geometry suffices</Item>
    <Item>Use sub-assemblies to manage complexity</Item>
    <Item>Employ lightweight or envelope modes for standard components</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Bike Lock Example:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Suppress detailed threading on screws during assembly work</Item>
    <Item>Use simplified cylinder representation (no internal pins) for top-level assembly</Item>
    <Item>Create detailed sub-assembly only when working on lock mechanism</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Establish Drawing Templates</strong></h4>
<p><p>Drawing templates are CAD drawing files that store preferred settings and can include layer standards, plot configurations, annotation styles, and many other details, creating a method of standardizing drawing features with little to no effort.</p>
<strong>Template Elements:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Title blocks with company information</Item>
    <Item>Standard views and projections (1st or 3rd angle)</Item>
    <Item>Default tolerances (per ASME Y14.5)</Item>
    <Item>Standard note blocks</Item>
    <Item>Layer structures</Item>
    <Item>Dimension styles</Item>
    <Item>Line weights and types</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Build Detail Libraries</strong></h4>
<p><p>Standardized detail libraries are key to success as this single innovation in production practice can yield a massive return on investment in terms of wasted billable hours, resulting in increased effectiveness.</p>
<strong>Standard Components Library:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Fasteners (screws, bolts, washers)</Item>
    <Item>Bearings and bushings</Item>
    <Item>O-rings and seals</Item>
    <Item>Springs</Item>
    <Item>Standard electronic components</Item>
    <Item>Common purchased parts</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Benefits:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Eliminates redundant modeling</Item>
    <Item>Ensures consistency across projects</Item>
    <Item>Reduces design time</Item>
    <Item>Maintains accurate supplier information</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Maintain CAD Standards</strong></h4>
<p><p>CAD standards are a set of guidelines for the appearance of computer-aided design drawings and for how CAD data is organized, intended to improve productivity and facilitate the interchange of information between different organizations and computer platforms.</p>
<strong>Key CAD Standards:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item><strong>ISO 128</strong> - Technical drawings standards (widely used internationally)</Item>
    <Item><strong>ISO 13567</strong> - Layer organization and terminology</Item>
    <Item><strong>ASME Y14</strong> series - US engineering drawing standards</Item>
    <Item><strong>Company-specific standards</strong> - Tailored to organizational needs</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Bike Lock CAD Standards:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Units: Millimeters for all mechanical parts</Item>
    <Item>Decimal places: 2 places for non-critical, 3 for critical dimensions</Item>
    <Item>Material specification in part properties</Item>
    <Item>Origin location: Bottom-left-front corner for prismatic parts, geometric center for cylindrical parts</Item>
    <Item>Feature naming: Descriptive and consistent</Item>
    <Item>Configurations: Clearly labeled (e.g., "Left-Hand" vs "Right-Hand")</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Component 2: Detailed Engineering Drawings" id="Component 2: Detailed Engineering Drawings">
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Purpose of Engineering Drawings" id="Purpose of Engineering Drawings">
<p>Engineers need to understand engineering drawings (also known as technical diagrams, blueprints, prints, mechanical or manufacturing drawings, or schematics) to effectively understand project development, outcomes, and functions. Engineering drawings provide:</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Complete dimensional specification</Item>
    <Item>Tolerance requirements</Item>
    <Item>Material specifications</Item>
    <Item>Surface finish requirements</Item>
    <Item>Manufacturing and assembly instructions</Item>
    <Item>Inspection criteria</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Drawing Standards - ASME Y14 Series" id="Drawing Standards - ASME Y14 Series">
<p>ASME Y14.5-2018 and ASME Y14.1-2020 establish uniform practices for stating and interpreting dimensioning, tolerancing, and related requirements for use on engineering drawings to assure consistency, clarity, and communication across teams and organizations.</p>
<h4><strong>ASME Y14.5 - Geometric Dimensioning & Tolerancing</strong></h4>
<p><p>The Y14.5 standard is considered the authoritative guideline for the design language of geometric dimensioning and tolerancing, establishing symbols, rules, definitions, requirements, defaults, and recommended practices.</p>
<strong>GD&T provides:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Precise geometric control of features</Item>
    <Item>Clear definition of datum reference frames</Item>
    <Item>Consistent interpretation across organizations</Item>
    <Item>Reduced ambiguity in manufacturing</Item>
    <Item>Improved quality and reduced costs</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Key GD&T Elements:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item><strong>Form tolerances:</strong> Flatness, straightness, circularity, cylindricity</Item>
    <Item><strong>Orientation tolerances:</strong> Perpendicularity, parallelism, angularity</Item>
    <Item><strong>Location tolerances:</strong> Position, concentricity, symmetry</Item>
    <Item><strong>Profile tolerances:</strong> Profile of a line, profile of a surface</Item>
    <Item><strong>Runout tolerances:</strong> Circular runout, total runout</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Bike Lock GD&T Example:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Lock body mounting face: Flatness within 0.1mm (ensures proper seal)</Item>
    <Item>Shackle hole: Position tolerance ±0.2mm relative to datum reference frame (ensures shackle fits)</Item>
    <Item>Cylinder bore: Cylindricity within 0.05mm (ensures smooth key operation)</Item>
    <Item>Key slot: Profile tolerance to ensure proper key engagement</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>ASME Y14.1 - Drawing Organization</strong></h4>
<p><p>ASME Y14.1 defines the general requirements for creating and interpreting engineering drawings, helping assure consistency across teams and companies.</p>
<strong>Drawing organization includes:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Standard sheet sizes (A through E)</Item>
    <Item>Title block information</Item>
    <Item>Drawing scale</Item>
    <Item>View arrangement</Item>
    <Item>Section view conventions</Item>
    <Item>Detail view standards</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Drawing Best Practices" id="Drawing Best Practices">
<h4><strong>Convey Design Intent Clearly</strong></h4>
<p><p>Remember that the purpose of the drawing is to specify geometry in such a way that the design intent of the part is preserved despite the natural variability of all manufacturing processes.</p>
<strong>Clear Communication:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Use appropriate number of views (not too few, not excessive)</Item>
    <Item>Apply section views to show internal features</Item>
    <Item>Include detail views for small features</Item>
    <Item>Add isometric views for complex geometry</Item>
    <Item>Write clear, concise notes</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Use Section and Detail Views Effectively</strong></h4>
<p><p>Use as many section views, detail views, and pages as necessary to convey design and manufacturing intent without ambiguity, but nothing more.</p>
<strong>Section Views:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Show internal features not visible in standard views</Item>
    <Item>Use appropriate section line patterns for different materials</Item>
    <Item>Label sections clearly (SECTION A-A)</Item>
    <Item>Use partial sections when full sections aren't needed</Item>

</Itemize>
<p><strong>Detail Views:</strong> Detail views apply local scaling of features to allow better feature and dimension legibility.</p>
<strong>Bike Lock Drawing Example:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Section A-A: Through lock body showing cylinder bore and internal mechanism space</Item>
    <Item>Section B-B: Through shackle showing hardened outer layer and core material</Item>
    <Item>Detail C (Scale 2:1): Keyway profile showing precise geometry</Item>
    <Item>Detail D (Scale 3:1): Seal groove dimensions</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Set Appropriate Tolerances</strong></h4>
<p><p>Set up a good title block for your company with appropriate default tolerances, as over-specifying tight tolerances is a path to wasting money and driving manufacturers crazy.</p>
<strong>Tolerance Guidelines:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Establish sensible default tolerances in title block</Item>
    <Item>Tighten tolerances only where functionally necessary</Item>
    <Item>Consider manufacturing capabilities</Item>
    <Item>Balance cost vs. performance requirements</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Bike Lock Tolerance Example:</strong>
<p>Default Tolerances (in title block):</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Linear dimensions: ±0.5mm</Item>
    <Item>Angular dimensions: ±1°</Item>
    <Item>Hole diameters: ±0.2mm</Item>

</Itemize>
<p>Critical Tolerances (specified on drawing):</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Cylinder bore: Ø15.00 +0.02/-0.00 mm (tight for proper fit)</Item>
    <Item>Shackle hole position: ±0.1mm (critical for function)</Item>
    <Item>Mounting face flatness: 0.05mm (sealing surface)</Item>

</Itemize>
<p>Non-critical Tolerances (use defaults):</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Overall body length: 65mm ±0.5mm (appearance only)</Item>
    <Item>Corner radii: R2mm ±0.5mm (not functional)</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>Write Clear Drawing Notes</strong></h4>
<p><p>Write simple and direct notes that clarify any ambiguity in the drawing or add extra information to convey design intent.</p>
<strong>Effective Notes:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Material specifications: "MATERIAL: AISI 4140 STEEL, HEAT TREAT TO 58-62 HRC"</Item>
    <Item>Surface finish: "SURFACE ROUGHNESS: Ra 0.8μm MAX ON SEALING SURFACES"</Item>
    <Item>Processing: "DEBURR ALL EDGES 0.2mm MAX RADIUS"</Item>
    <Item>Assembly: "APPLY LOCTITE 243 TO THREADS DURING ASSEMBLY"</Item>
    <Item>Special features: "DRILL Ø6.5mm, TAP M8x1.25 - 6H"</Item>

</Itemize>
