<p>\end{verbatim}</p> -->
<h4><strong>Understand Line Types</strong></h4>
<p><p>Modern CAD tools automate much of the line-work, but it helps to have a basic understanding of the various line types used across drawing views.</p>
<strong>Standard Line Types:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item><strong>Continuous solid</strong> (thick): Visible edges and surfaces</Item>
    <Item><strong>Hidden line</strong> (dashed): Features obscured by other geometry</Item>
    <Item><strong>Center line</strong> (thin, long-short-long): Axes of symmetry, bolt circles</Item>
    <Item><strong>Center mark</strong>: Center point of holes and cylindrical features</Item>
    <Item><strong>Section line</strong> (thin, solid): Cut surface hatching</Item>
    <Item><strong>Break line</strong>: Shortened views of long features</Item>
    <Item><strong>Phantom line</strong> (thin, long-short-short-long): Adjacent parts, alternate positions</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Drawing Template Setup" id="Drawing Template Setup">
<p>Whatever CAD software you use, be sure to set up drawing sheet templates in the common sizes you will use, including appropriate default tolerances, references to standards, and drawing borders with alphanumeric grid lines.</p>
<strong>Template Components:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item><strong>Title block:</strong> Company name, logo, drawing number, revision, date, approvals</Item>
    <Item><strong>Revision block:</strong> Track design changes</Item>
    <Item><strong>General notes section:</strong> Standard requirements applicable to all drawings</Item>
    <Item><strong>Material/finish callout areas</strong></Item>
    <Item><strong>Drawing scale indicator</strong></Item>
    <Item><strong>Projection type:</strong> 1st angle (Europe) or 3rd angle (US)</Item>
    <Item><strong>Standard reference:</strong> "INTERPRET DIMENSIONS PER ASME Y14.5-2018"</Item>

</Itemize>
