<h4><strong>7. Specify Make vs. Buy</strong></h4>
<p><p>Provide procurement information including how materials are procured - whether bought pre-fabricated, custom-produced for your business, or made in-house.</p>
<strong>Make vs. Buy Decision Factors:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Core competency (can we make it well?)</Item>
    <Item>Cost (make vs. purchase)</Item>
    <Item>Quality control requirements</Item>
    <Item>Lead time</Item>
    <Item>Production capacity</Item>
    <Item>Intellectual property considerations</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Bike Lock Make/Buy:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item><strong>MAKE:</strong> Lock body (die casting capability in-house)</Item>
    <Item><strong>MAKE:</strong> Shackle (machining capability in-house)</Item>
    <Item><strong>BUY:</strong> Lock cylinder (specialized vendor)</Item>
    <Item><strong>BUY:</strong> Springs (standard vendor)</Item>
    <Item><strong>BUY:</strong> Fasteners (catalog items)</Item>
    <Item><strong>CUSTOM BUY:</strong> Weather seals (custom tooling at vendor)</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>8. Integrate with CAD</strong></h4>
<p><p>Live BOM natively integrated into CAD software ensures adherence to engineering BOM best practices and provides engineers visibility into their supply chain.</p>
<strong>CAD-BOM Integration Benefits:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Automatic BOM generation from assembly</Item>
    <Item>Real-time updates when design changes</Item>
    <Item>Reduced manual data entry errors</Item>
    <Item>Direct access to supplier data</Item>
    <Item>Easier design optimization based on cost</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Implementation:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Populate CAD part properties (part number, description, material)</Item>
    <Item>Use CAD configurations for variants</Item>
    <Item>Export BOM directly from CAD assembly</Item>
    <Item>Validate exported BOM against requirements</Item>

</Itemize>
<h4><strong>9. Maintain BOM Accuracy</strong></h4>
<p><p><strong>Accuracy is critical:</strong> An effective BOM ensures that all individual parts and components necessary for the final product are documented, reducing the risk of missing items during assembly.</p>
<strong>Verification Steps:</strong></p> 

<Itemize>

    <Item>Cross-check BOM against CAD assembly</Item>
    <Item>Verify quantities by counting in 3D model</Item>
    <Item>Confirm part numbers match drawings</Item>
    <Item>Validate material specifications</Item>
    <Item>Check supplier part numbers</Item>
    <Item>Review with manufacturing team</Item>
    <Item>Physical prototype verification (build one unit from BOM)</Item>

</Itemize>
<strong>Common BOM Errors to Avoid:</strong>
<Itemize>

    <Item>Missing consumables (adhesives, lubricants)</Item>
    <Item>Incorrect quantities</Item>
    <Item>Wrong part numbers</Item>
    <Item>Missing hardware (screws, washers)</Item>
    <Item>Omitted packaging materials</Item>
    <Item>Obsolete part numbers</Item>
    <Item>Incorrect units of measure</Item>

</Itemize>
